<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Paratarajas</h1><p class="st-meta">125 CE – 300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An obscure dynasty of Balochistan, known almost only from its coins, which ruled on the borderlands of Iran and India.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Paratarajas, kings of the Paratas or Paradas, ruled in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a> from around the 2nd century. They are known mainly from coins with legends in Brahmi and Kharoshthi that name a sequence of kings and their fathers, and they appear to have been connected to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indo-parthian-kingdom">Indo-Parthian Kingdom</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins show the king's bust on one side and a swastika on the other, with titles that call the ruler Paratarajas. Kings such as Yolamira and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> are known only by name. The state probably controlled routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Paratarajas likely came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> in the 3rd century; the Paikuli inscription of the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narseh">Narseh</a> mentions a ruler of the Paradan among his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. Their coinage ends around 300.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/paratarajas.jpg" alt="Paratarajas" width="320" height="350"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Paratarajas_ruler_Kozana_circa_200-220_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>125 CE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yolamira</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Parataraja kingdom, Paradas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=212&amp;focus=paratarajas" aria-label="Paratarajas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6467 1098 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Paratarajas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6357 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6787 1354h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6787 1354h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Pakistan. <a href="/atlas?year=212&amp;focus=paratarajas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

